[FINAL-TEXT] I now have the complete routing picture across all path nodes. Let me synthesise the findings before writing the final diagnosis:

**Summary of evidence:**
- `router1`: `ripd` **absent** (only `zebra/mgmtd/staticd` running); FRR config **completely empty** (no `router rip`); routing table has only two directly-connected subnets + two static routes to 172.18.50/51 via r2.
- All peers (`router0`, `router2`–`router6`) run `ripd` with proper `router rip` + network statements.
- **Return-path routing loop confirmed:** r6 routes 172.16.10.0/24 and 192.168.200.0/24 → r3 (via RIP); r3 routes those same prefixes → r4 (via RIP metric 20); r4 only has `default via r3` (static) → back to r3. Packets loop r3↔r4 until TTL expires.
- **Why this loop exists only when r1's ripd is down:** When ripd ran on r1, r2 learned 172.16.10.0/24 and 192.168.200.0/24 from r1 at a shorter RIP metric, and r3 used that r2→r1→r0 path. Without r1's RIP advertisements, r3 falls back to the ring path via r4, whose static default creates the loop.
- **Forward path still works** via the static routes pre-installed on r0→r1→r2→r3→r6, but replies never return — hence pure timeout (no ICMP unreachable).

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The RIP routing daemon (`ripd`) on router1, along with its entire FRR RIP configuration.

**Mechanism:** The network uses RIP v2 (FRR's `ripd`) as its routing protocol across all seven routers. Router1 sits on the direct transit spine between router0 (left zone: 172.16.10.x hosts) and router2 (which feeds the r3/r6/mswitch2 cluster serving the 172.18.51.x zone). When ripd was running on router1, router2 learned 172.16.10.0/24 and 192.168.200.0/24 from router1 at a short metric (22 hops), and router3 in turn used router2 for those prefixes. Without router1's RIP participation, router3 loses the shorter path and falls back to learning those prefixes via router4 (metric 20 via the ring). Router4, however, has only a static default route pointing back to router3, creating a TTL-expiring routing loop: router3 → router4 → router3 → .... Forward traffic to 172.18.51.x still reaches pc15 via pre-installed static routes on r0→r1→r2→r3→r6, but ICMP replies are black-holed in the r3/r4 loop, causing every cross-zone ping to time out with no ICMP unreachable.

**Localization:** router1, FRR daemon layer. Process list shows only `zebra`, `mgmtd`, and `staticd` — `ripd` is not present. `vtysh show running-config` returns a bare FRR header with no `router rip` block and no interface or network statements; all peer routers carry full `router rip` configurations.

**Root cause:** `ripd` was stopped and its configuration was deleted from router1, breaking RIP convergence across the entire transit spine and inducing a return-path routing loop between router3 and router4.

**Fix:** Restore the `router rip` configuration on router1 (adding `network` statements for the wan_link_r0_r1 subnet 10.16.1.0/24 and wan_link_r1_r2 subnet 10.20.1.0/24, plus any redistributed prefixes), enable `ripd` in `/etc/frr/daemons`, and restart FRR on router1. Once RIP adjacencies re-establish with router0 and router2, router3 will re-learn the shorter path via router2→router1 and the loop dissolves.
=== END ===